  - Costs: manual DOM updates get tedious past a few interactive surfaces, and there's no component model if the UI
    grows.

  [1mB. Vite plus a small framework[0m (Preact or Svelte). Component model, reactive state, [38;5;153mvitest[39m for tests out of the box.

  - Wins if you expect the UI to get genuinely rich — filters, drag-and-drop reordering, an eventual notification inbox
    with live updates.
  - Costs: a build toolchain for what is currently a list of three-field records, and more infrastructure in cycle 1
    than the feature justifies.

  [1mC. Web Components, no build.[0m Custom elements ([38;5;153m<task-list>[39m, [38;5;153m<task-item>[39m) with native ES modules.

  - Wins if you want component boundaries without a bundler.
  - Costs: more ceremony per component than A, awkward styling through shadow DOM, and a weaker testing story than
    either alternative.

  [1mI recommend A.[0m The storage interface is the boundary that actually matters here, and it's orthogonal to all three — so
  the framework choice buys less than it normally would. Cycle 4's inbox is the only thing that might want B, and by
  then you'll know whether it needs live updates. Going A now and adding a build step later is a contained change;
  carrying a toolchain through three cycles to maybe need it in the fourth is not.

  I'd rule out C: it has B's ceremony without B's payoff.
